# MicroSkope @VERSION@

A customized NifSkope build. Existing NifSkope 2.0 settings are retained.

- V / W / E / R select Select / Move / Rotate / Scale in the viewport.
- Hold RMB to look around; RMB + WASD moves, Q/E goes down/up, Shift moves
  faster, and the wheel adjusts fly speed. Release RMB to resume editing.
- Ctrl+L selects all connected vertices or faces from the current selection.
- Vertex/face snapping uses only the axis or plane of the active Move handle.
- The UV editor supports box selection and a transform cage: drag the interior
  to move, square handles to scale, and the round handle to rotate. Shift keeps
  proportions on corner scaling or snaps rotation to 15 degrees. Escape cancels.

## Based on NifSkope

NifSkope is a tool for opening and editing the NetImmerse file format (NIF). NIF is used by video games such as Morrowind, Oblivion, Skyrim, Fallout 3, Fallout: New Vegas, Civilization IV, and more. 

### Download

You can download the latest official release from [GitHub](https://github.com/niftools/nifskope/releases). More frequent development builds are posted in the [NifTools Discord](https://discord.gg/ZFjdN4x), pinned to #software.


### Discussion & Help

In the Block List, Ctrl-click to select or deselect individual blocks, or Shift-click to select a range. The current block controls the Block Details pane and viewport; block actions apply to the current block.

Use **Move**, **Rotate**, or **Scale** on the Render toolbar (or Render > Transform) to edit the current node or shape in the viewport. Drag a red, green, or blue arrow to move along the local X, Y, or Z axis; drag a ring to rotate around that axis. Scale uses the yellow center handle: drag right/up to enlarge or left/down to shrink, uniformly on all axes. Choose **Select** to hide the gizmo. Drag outside the handles to orbit as usual; Escape cancels an active drag. Pause animation playback before transforming objects or vertices.

For vertex editing, select a mesh in **Object Select**, then choose **Vertex Select**. LMB drag-box selects vertices through the locked mesh; Shift adds, Ctrl toggles, and Ctrl+A selects all. Use Move, Rotate, or Scale to transform the selection around its center. Enable **Snap Vertices** to snap the nearest selected anchor to another vertex under the pointer during Move; the target can belong to another visible mesh. Space+LMB pans and Alt+LMB orbits. Escape cancels a selection box or transform. Return to Object Select to choose another mesh.

This first vertex editor supports static, unskinned meshes: classic geometry, supported Bethesda vertex arrays, and NiMesh float32/float16 position streams with one contiguous region. Shared geometry is copied for independent editing. Skinned meshes and morph/deformation modifiers show an explanatory message. UVs, colors, indices, and stored normals are preserved; normals are not recalculated automatically. Saved bounds update after a completed vertex transform.

Click a color's hex value in the property table to edit it directly. Copy/paste with Ctrl+C/Ctrl+V; Enter applies and Escape cancels. RGB fields accept six hex digits with or without `#`, in either case. RGBA fields also accept eight digits in `#RRGGBBAA` order; pasting six digits preserves their existing alpha. Incomplete/invalid codes cannot overwrite the stored color. Opening/copying an unchanged value preserves the original float precision and HDR values. The color-wheel icon still opens its existing picker. Color changes support Undo/Redo.

With **Show Nodes** enabled, lights have bright indicators that remain visible through geometry. Directional lights show an arrow in their emission direction; spotlights show an arrow and their cone angles; point lights show an omnidirectional globe. Arrows follow Gamebryo's local +X light axis, including parent rotations. Selected lights turn orange. These fixed-size guides show direction and cone angle, not light range, and never change NIF data. Click a marker's center in Object Select to select the light, then move or rotate it with the gizmo. Show Nodes off hides the indicators and disables their picking.

Selecting a point or spot light with **Show Nodes** enabled also displays world-sized distance falloff guides: solid at 10% attenuation and dashed at 1%. The viewport shows their distances in world units. Gamebryo defines attenuation as `1 / (C + L*d + Q*d*d)`, where `d` is world-space distance and C/L/Q are Constant/Linear/Quadratic Attenuation. All three terms count; zero terms contribute nothing. For C=0, L=0, Q=0.000016, the guides are approximately 790.6 and 2500 units from the light. Larger positive L or Q reduces the distances. These are attenuation multipliers relative to the unattenuated light, not percentages of the displayed peak brightness, and are not hard cutoff radii. Color, dimmer, spotlight angular falloff, material lighting and game-specific shaders affect the visible result. Constant-only and all-zero attenuation have no finite decay contour; invalid negative/nonfinite values are not visualized. Parent scale changes the light's position but does not multiply its world-space falloff radius. The guides update when properties change and on Undo/Redo, never modify saved data, and appear only for the selected light. Directional lights have no distance falloff radius.

In the lighting popover (bulb icon), enable **Unshaded geometry (keep textures)** to remove geometry lighting, normal-map shading, specular and vertex RGB shading. For meshes with a base texture, it ignores material diffuse RGB while preserving material opacity. It preserves the existing texture compositing, including lightmaps, detail maps, decals, glow, UV sets and transparency. Turning it off restores normal rendering and leaves the other preview settings unchanged. The setting is remembered and applies to workspace reference layers too; it never changes NIF data. **Lightmap/detail brightness** is a viewport-only slider from 0% to 200%, with a percentage box for precise values. 100% matches the previous 1x limit; 200% restores the original 2x brightness. It remembers the value, carries over the old checkbox preference, and also applies to workspace references. Saving/exporting NIF data and textures is unaffected.

Enable **Mesh Center Pivot** on the render toolbar or Render menu to place the object gizmo automatically at the selected mesh's bounding-box center. Object rotation and scaling use that temporary center; disabling it returns the gizmo to the stored origin. Changing pivot mode does not edit the NIF or rebase its vertices. Vertex mode continues to pivot around the selected vertices. Clicking a vertex selects every vertex at exactly the same XYZ position in the active mesh, including duplicates at UV/normal seams; vertices that only overlap on screen are not grouped.

The **Move** gizmo includes square handles for moving across the local XY, XZ, and YZ planes; arrows still move along one axis. Plane handles work on objects and selected vertices, with one undo step per drag and Escape to cancel. Nearly edge-on plane handles are hidden. **Walk** mode supports RMB-held WASD camera navigation and allows gizmo editing. Switching into Walk preserves the current camera position and selection instead of recentering inside the selected object; clicking empty space while walking retains the selection.

Use **Workspace > Add NIF to Workspace...** to open a second NIF alongside the current file. File tabs appear above the viewport; click a tab to choose the editable NIF. Other files are transparent, click-through references at their original world coordinates. **Workspace > Show Reference NIFs** and **Reference opacity** control the overlay. Switching files keeps the camera and restores each file's selected mesh/vertices and independent undo history. With multiple files open, normal **Save / Ctrl+S** and **Workspace > Save All NIFs / Ctrl+Shift+S** save each NIF to its own path. PhysX collision rebuilding remains explicit on the collision tab. Save All stages every file before replacing any destination; write failures are reported and unsaved edits are retained. Close a file's tab to remove it from the workspace without deleting its disk file. A single open NIF keeps the normal layout and save behavior. The workspace file list is session-only; the NIFs themselves remain independent files.

Select a mesh and click **UV Editor** on the render toolbar or Render menu (or right-click the mesh and choose **Texture > Edit UV**). NiMesh float32/float16 UV streams are supported alongside the existing classic/Bethesda mesh paths. Left-click selects and drags UVs; drag a box from empty space to select several, use Shift to add, Ctrl+A to select all, middle-drag to pan, the wheel to zoom, and F to frame the mapping. Right-click inside the editor for scale/translate, rotation, coordinate-set selection, or loading a DDS/TGA/BMP background texture when the original cannot be found. Changes appear in the main document and save normally. Ctrl+Z/Ctrl+Y use document undo/redo; each UV drag is one undo step and the editor remains open when undoing a UV edit. NiMesh support currently requires one triangle-list submesh and contiguous UV/index regions; shared UV buffers are refused to avoid changing another object's mapping. UV writes preserve the other components of interleaved streams.

For MicroVolts static PhysX triangle collision, **Show Collision** displays the stored collision as a cyan wireframe, with the selected source highlighted in orange. Click a collision edge in Object Select to select its matching source mesh or group. Select individual child meshes for vertex editing. Enable **Show Hidden** if the source meshes are hidden, then use Vertex Select and the transform tools to edit them. Object transforms also work. Click **Rebuild Collision** on the render toolbar (or Render menu), select the game's `Bin/PhysXCooking.dll` on first use, and save the NIF. Each collider needs a uniquely named NiMesh, or a named NiNode whose static NiMesh descendants are combined. Rebuilding uses PhysX 2.8.4 and validates every mesh with the game runtime before replacing any collision data. The entire rebuild is one undo step. Neither vertex edits nor saving automatically rebuild collision; the button is disabled for files without NiPhysXProp.

This initial collision editor supports static triangle meshes with one state. Convex shapes, dynamic/parented actors, joints, cloth, mixed per-face materials, and shared collision mesh descriptions are rejected without changing the document. Other collision formats remain on their existing code paths. On Windows, keep `PhysxCooker.exe` beside NifSkope; qmake builds this small x86 helper with the .NET Framework compiler. The game's PhysX DLLs are not bundled or changed. The supplied player-collision sample passes cooking, runtime loading, coordinate/winding preservation, save/reload and undo tests; gameplay still needs testing in MicroVolts.

**Ctrl+Z** undoes document edits, including block deletion, block/branch paste, field and array edits, and gizmo transforms. **Ctrl+Y** redoes them. Each gizmo drag or block action is one undo step; history retains the most recent 50 steps and resets when another document is loaded.

- Visit the [NifTools Discord](https://discord.gg/ZFjdN4x). To receive support use the #software channel.
- Visit the [NifTools.org](https://forum.niftools.org/) forum. To receive support for NifSkope please use the [Support subforum](https://forum.niftools.org/24-nifskope/).

### Issues

Anyone can [report issues at GitHub](https://github.com/niftools/nifskope/issues) or in the NifTools.org [Support subforum](https://forum.niftools.org/24-nifskope/).


### Contribute

You can fork the latest source from [GitHub](https://github.com/niftools/nifskope). See [Fork A Repo](https://help.github.com/articles/fork-a-repo) on how to send your contributions upstream. To grab all submodules, make sure to use `--recursive` like so:

```
git clone --recursive git://github.com/<YOUR_USERNAME>/nifskope.git
```

For information about development:

- Visit our [Discord #dev channel](https://discord.gg/zvWZrrJ).
- Visit the NifTools.org [development subforum](https://forum.niftools.org/6-nifskope-development/).
- Refer to our [GitHub wiki](https://github.com/niftools/nifskope/wiki#wiki-development) for information on compilation.  


