Face editing: select a mesh in Object mode, then choose Faces in the render
toolbar. Click selects the nearest triangle; drag selects triangle centers
through the mesh. Shift adds and Ctrl toggles selection. Move, Rotate and Scale
transform the selected triangles' vertices; adjacent faces sharing those vertices
follow the edit. Delete or Delete Selection removes selected triangles. Ctrl+A selects
all faces. Face deletion and transforms support Undo/Redo. The current face editor
supports static NiTriShape meshes and single-submesh NiMesh triangle lists with
contiguous index regions. Shared geometry is copied automatically when entering edit mode on a duplicate. Vertex selection mode displays the mesh edges as an X-ray wireframe.

Make Material Unique copies the selected mesh's material/property dependencies
and gives its material a free numbered name (FOOTING_01 becomes FOOTING_02;
FOOTING becomes FOOTING_01). Use it after duplicating a mesh to edit its material
and texture settings independently. New Material assigns a default material to
the selected mesh. Both controls are in the render toolbar and Render menu, and
are also available under Spells > Mesh. Texture image files remain on disk; these
actions copy the NIF's settings and references.

Press Tab over the viewport to cycle Object > Vertex > Face > Object. Use
**Shift+X** over the viewport (or Mesh Tools > X-Ray Selection) to show/select through geometry, or turn it off for
visible geometry only. Delete (or Delete Selection) removes selected vertices
and their connected faces in Vertex mode; remaining attributes and triangle
indices are compacted together. These edits support Undo/Redo.

The selection-mode button cycles Object / Vertex / Face; its arrow opens the
mode list. The transform button cycles Select / Move / Rotate / Scale, with
V/W/E/R shortcuts and an arrow menu. Both show the active icon. Mesh Tools groups
Delete Selection, Snap Vertices, Mesh Center Pivot and X-Ray Selection; Materials
groups material creation and uniqueness. UV Editor and Rebuild Collision remain
separate icon buttons. Replace the placeholder PNGs in `res/icon/editor/` and
rebuild to customize these icons.

A swift RMB click (up to 250 ms with at most four pixels of pointer travel) opens
the viewport context menu. Holding RMB, looking around, using a movement key, or
adjusting fly speed suppresses it. Native context events are blocked so a menu
cannot interrupt navigation or open twice.

Materials > Material Editor opens a dock for the selected mesh. Choose an existing
material and Assign, or use New + Assign to create and attach one immediately.
Make Unique detaches shared materials before editing. Name, ambient/diffuse/
specular/emissive colors, opacity and glossiness are editable; assignment also
uses the source mesh's texture and alpha properties when available. Opacity
rendering follows the mesh's alpha/blending settings. These changes are undoable.

N opens relative numeric Move/Rotate/uniform Scale controls for objects or selected
vertices/faces, in local or world space. Mesh Tools > World Axes also aligns the
gizmo to world axes. Shift+H isolates the selected mesh/subtree; Alt+H restores
other meshes without changing saved visibility. The UV editor and viewport now
synchronize vertex/face selections for the active mesh.
The material sidebar includes texture map slots with editable paths and Browse
buttons. Apply Material Changes adds/replaces maps; clearing a path removes the
map. Slots follow the file version (unsupported maps are disabled). Assign the
chosen material first; map edits affect the selected mesh and copy shared texture
descriptors/sources as needed. File browsing stores paths relative to the NIF.

The UV editor's Texture dropdown lists the selected mesh's material maps. Choosing
Base, Detail/lightmap or another map displays its texture and selects the UV
channel assigned by that slot, including packed NiMesh texture flags. UV labels
are one-based (UV 1, UV 2). The UV dropdown can preview another channel without
changing the material assignment. Undo keeps the active texture and UV channel.

Ctrl+I inverts the active mesh's selection in Vertex or Face mode. In the UV
editor it inverts UV selection. Selection inversion does not edit mesh data.

The UV editor stays owned by the main window and follows mesh selection in Object
mode. Vertex/face selections synchronize in both directions, with exact viewport
face highlights. Selecting non-mesh nodes keeps the current UV window open;
geometry detachment and undo no longer close it.

Cube Unwrap in the UV editor projects selected complete faces (or the entire mesh
when no faces are selected) into six padded atlas regions on the active UV channel.
Seams split vertices and copy their other attributes/UV channels. Supported static
NiMesh triangle lists and NiTriShape meshes retain their shape and support undo.

All UV editor entry points (toolbar and Texture > Edit UV) share the same owned
window and viewport-selection synchronization. Texture/UV controls and Cube
Unwrap sit in a separate toolbar above the OpenGL canvas. Cube Unwrap is also
available in the UV editor context menu.

Render > Rooms opens the existing-room inspector. Select and move a static mesh
normally; its room membership is unchanged until Assign Selected Mesh to Room is
clicked. Choose Suggested Room selects a destination only when one room fully
contains the mesh. The sidebar also offers room/portal boundary overlays and a
read-only structural link check. Reassignment preserves world transforms and
render properties and supports undo/redo. Ambiguous/shared ownership, external
references, occluder tags, animation, incompatible inherited state and cross-group
transfers are blocked with an explanation. See docs/room-workflow.md.

Collision source groups are resolved through Children links, including material
splits such as coc_sand:0 and coc_sand:2. Child names do not need consecutive
suffixes. Source ancestry transforms and mirrored winding are preserved while
merging into the existing collider; the scene hierarchy and materials are kept.
The rebuild status names the active NIF and lists grouped source contributions.
Each collision NIF remains independent; rebuild and save the file used by the
game for the collision being tested. Already loaded game actors require reloading
their collision; fully restarting the game avoids cached mesh-name reuse.

Render > Rooms > Portal Openings edits existing openings. Choose a portal, select
boundary vertices or planar faces in Vertex/Face mode, and click Preview Fit
Rectangle to Selection. The selected portal is yellow; proposed outlines are
magenta. Apply Portal Change commits the preview; Cancel Preview leaves the file
unchanged. Move U/V and Scale U/V offer a separate numeric move/resize preview.
Matching reverse portals update together by default, with opposite winding and
correct local transforms. Missing/ambiguous reverse matches require review or an
explicit one-way edit. Fitting keeps the original divider plane and reports any
projection of a parallel selected face. Room bounds, portal links and flags stay
unchanged. See docs/portal-workflow.md.
